1: # Irreducible type II caps in the primitive source profiles
2: 
3: Working local and integral proof for audit, 2026-11-17. No return
4: index is assigned. All existing manuscript TeX remains unchanged.
5: 
6: The profiles of P3K.22 with a type II fibre and a retained I1 are
7: I8+II+2I1 (row45), I7+I2+II+I1 (row47), and I5+I4+II+I1
8: (row55). Their primitive data are M=O(dG-O),Q=G with d=8,14,20.
9: Take the II site as the sole logarithmic cap and a retained I1
10: as the sole simple whole-fibre scalar zero. All other components
11: and the whole-fibre Hom degree one remain exactly those in K22.
12: 
13: ## II.1. Actual roots and the section marking
14: 
15: Write the actual minimal II equation as
16: 
17:     y^2=x^3+q a(q)x+q b(q), b(0) nonzero.
18: 
19: For q=z^m,x=z^(2l)X,y=z^(3l)Y, the two admissible residues are
20: 
21:     m=6l+1: Y^2=X^3+z^(2l+1)a(z^m)X+z b(z^m),
22:     m=6l:   Y^2=X^3+z^(2l)a(z^m)X+b(z^m).
23: 
24: The first is a reduced II fibre in a smooth total surface. The
25: second is good, with central curve Y^2=X^3+b(0) and deck
26: (X,Y)->(zeta_3^(-1)X,-Y). Its order-six automorphism fixes only O:
27: a finite fixed point would have both X=0 and Y=0, contrary to
28: b(0) nonzero. Consequently every original marked section,
29: including Q=G and dG, has root limit O. In the exact root
30: coordinate w=-X/Y, its expansion is w=z^l c(z^m), since the
31: minimal sixth root has weight one. Thus the central good-root
32: norm requires nQ0=0 and is satisfied here. It is not the
33: nonzero-torsion norm occurring at primitive III/IV sites.
34: 
35: For the intermediate residue m=6l+2, k=l gives a reduced IV
36: root. In X=zX1,Y=zY1 the deck character on Y1 is
37: zeta_m^(-(3l+1))=-1. It fixes the identity component and
38: interchanges the other two. Q remains on the identity
39: component: composing this root with its cubic good root gives
40: the same order-six fixed point O. The affine component norm
41: therefore cannot generate all three labels. The actual
42: reflection has two component orbits, and the compact
43: intersection pairing of P3K.19 excludes a sphere cap.
44: The residues three, four and five give the nonreduced I0*,
45: IV* and II* fibres, excluded by their unequal multiplicities
46: and that same proved intersection obstruction.
47: 
48: ## II.2. The good-root line correspondence
49: 
50: On the minimal sixth root, the central curve has one order-six
51: fixed point O, one orbit of two points with stabilizer order
52: three, and one orbit of three points with stabilizer order two.
53: At each point its stabilizer acts on the two surface coordinates
54: with weights (1,1). Blow up all six points. The quotient by
55: mu6 has central curve C of square -1 and three exceptional
56: curves D6,D3,D2 of squares -6,-3,-2. In the actual quotient,
57: 
58:     pi^*C=E_hat,
59:     pi^*D6=6E_O,
60:     pi^*D3=3(E_P1+E_P2),
61:     pi^*D2=2(E_R1+E_R2+E_R3).
62: 
63: Here E_hat has square -6 and maps with degree six. The central
64: fibre is 6C+D6+2D3+3D2. Contract C, then D2, then D3. Their
65: successive self-intersections are -1 at the moment of contraction.
66: After the first step the squares are -5,-2,-1 with pairwise
67: intersection one; after the second the remaining squares are
68: -4,-1 with intersection two; after the third D6 has square zero
69: and the cuspidal singularity specified by the invariant equation
70: y^2=x^3+q a(q)x+q b(q). Thus the displayed invariant map and
71: these contractions identify this quotient with the original
72: minimal II source, including its smooth total surface.
73: 
74: Both marked sections dG' and O pass through the root point O.
75: In the blowup chart w=z u they meet E_O at the finite marked
76: coordinates u=c_dG(q) and u=0. The proper central elliptic
77: curve meets E_O at u=infinity, so both sections avoid every
78: contracted curve. Each section has exceptional coefficient one
79: at this single blowup. Subtracting their divisors therefore
80: cancels that coefficient exactly and gives
81: 
82:     beta^*O(dG'-O) = pi^*alpha^*O(dG-O).             (II.1)
83: 
84: This identity also holds if the two sections have further
85: contact: both sides are differences of their actual Cartier
86: divisors in the displayed (u,q) chart. No other exceptional
87: point lies on either section. For m=6l pull back the complete
88: diagram and resolve its fibre product. The actual root source
89: translation Q' has limit O, fixes the six centres, and lifts
90: through the whole diagram. Thus (II.1) carries its actual
91: suspension line map B0 and its deck commutation to the line
92: N=O(dG'-O). On the good central fibre N is the trivial Picard
93: class, as required by its actual marked section limit.
94: 
95: At a reduced II root the pullback/relative-minimal line comparison
96: has only one irreducible component. Its vertical difference is
97: therefore a whole-fibre divisor. The corresponding base monomial
98: retains the complete deck character and supplies the extending
99: B0. This is the same actual divisor comparison as at a reduced
100: III/IV root, with a one-component intersection kernel.
101: 
102: ## II.3. Source norm, actual cap and the family closing
103: 
104: Choose gcd(m,n)=1. Multiplication by m has a unique inverse
105: branch at the marked central point: it acts on the additive
106: group at a II root, and on the elliptic group at O at a good
107: root. This gives a D-invariant analytic R with mR=Q'.
108: The source map a0=t_(-nR)D satisfies a0^m=t_(-nQ').
109: Lift t_(-R) to F on N using the actual chord divisor and its
110: whole-fibre correction. Normalize DFD^(-1)=chi F, chi^m=1.
111: Write B0=beta(q)F^m. Its actual deck descent makes beta
112: invariant. Then
113: 
114:     (F^n D)^m/B0^n=chi^(n m(m-1)/2) beta(q)^(-n).
115: 
116: Dividing by an invariant analytic mth root gives the commuting
117: line norm A0^m=B0^n. This retains the possible even-order
118: character factor. Set A_r=r^n A0,B_lambda=r^m B0. Bezout
119: gives H=A_r^u B_lambda^v=rH0 with H^m=B_lambda,H^n=A_r.
120: Strict norm contraction on a smaller compact tube constructs
121: the actual smooth proper cap N^times/<H> and proves delta
122: free. Its outward meridian maps are q->-nh,gamma->mh.
123: The ordinary-to-cap cochain and closing are precisely the
124: ones in SC.4/OA.2: scalar variation n(c-c0)s delta/m,
125: inverse closing (t-t_c)^(-n), lambda=r^m. They extend by
126: the actual scalar-zero cusp charts and by units elsewhere.
127: 
128: ## II.4. Every integral map for one II cap
129: 
130: The full source/boundary cones are executed in
131: code/part3/ii_cap_boundary.py/.json/.out. Here the removed
132: component column is L=F and its intersection matrix is zero.
133: The source-complement complex is C4=Z -> C3=Z -> C2=NS
134: -> C1=Z -> C0=Z with d4=1 and d2=F^t B. The boundary
135: complex has one Z in each degree zero through three and
136: zero differentials. Its actual translation and inclusion
137: are obtained by the same uncancelled cones as OA.3.
138: 
139: The source complement is simply connected. Its fundamental
140: group is a quotient of the elliptic lattice because the section
141: kills the base loops; F^tB is surjective, so its H1 vanishes.
142: Its translation coinvariants are free Z, generated by the
143: actual relative-Wang cycle T O-O, and its invariant lattice
144: is ZF. One can also read this directly from the exact sequence
145: 0 -> F-perp -> NS -> Z -> 0: in the K22 full Wang calculation
146: the invariant F maps to zero and the cokernel generator O
147: maps to one, forcing the relative-Wang column to be primitive.
148: 
149: Thus H1(W)=H2(W)=H3(W)=Z,H4(W)=0. The boundary E has
150: H1=H2=H3=Z^2,H4=Z. In the stored marked H2 basis the actual
151: fibre is primitive and its exterior image is zero; the other
152: boundary column is a unit. In both the reduced II and good
153: cap, H1=Zh,H2=ZC,H3=Z(C h),H4=0. For good reduction
154: the determinant of D-I on the elliptic lattice is one, so
155: there is no hidden finite cap group. The actual generic
156: fibre transport/specialization has degree one in either cap.
157: 
158: Consequently, retaining the integer collar coefficient kappa,
159: the full middle map is D2=[[0,1],[-1,-kappa]] after the
160: explicit oriented boundary choice. The other degree maps in
161: the actual bases (q,gamma) and (source boundary,F gamma) are
162: D1=D3=[[0,1],[n,-m]], while D4 is Z->0 and D0=(1,-1)^t.
163: The cap rows follow from u_cap=m u_ordinary-n arg(q)/(2pi);
164: there is no H3 source term because each cap source retracts
165: to its two-dimensional fibre. In the good case the actual
166: degree-one regular-fibre transport carries the two boundary
167: elliptic generators onto the cap's full normal elliptic Z^2,
168: which is killed in the simply connected source complement.
169: The reduced II cap source is already simply connected. The
170: remaining marked relations give pi1(Y)=Z/|n| by Van Kampen.
171: For n nonzero the integral homology is H0=H5=Z,
172: H1=H3=Z/|n|,H2=H4=0. Hence exactly n=+/-1 are spheres
173: in these one-cap domains. Their actual Hopf filling is the
174: standard S6 by the proved K22 Gysin calculation. No return
175: class has been assigned from this topology computation.
